{
 "id": "l01",
 "label": "1",
 "title": "The Tagalog alphabet",
 "summary": "Letters, vowels, the ng and r sounds, and how borrowed words are spelled",
 "minutes": 25,
 "goals": [
  "Know the letters of the alphabet and the five vowel sounds",
  "Make the special sounds ng, ñ and the tapped r",
  "Read the diphthongs ay, aw, ey, iw, oy and uy",
  "Decode Spanish and English loanwords in Tagalog spelling",
  "Learn the days of the week and months of the year"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "The letters",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog spelling is close to the way words sound. If you know the sounds of the letters, you can read almost any word aloud."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In the 1930s, when Tagalog was chosen as the basis for the national language, the alphabet had **20 letters**: five vowels and fifteen consonants, with **ng** counted as one letter."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Group",
      "Letters"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Vowels (5)",
       "a, e, i, o, u"
      ],
      [
       "Consonants (15)",
       "b, k, d, g, h, l, m, n, ng, p, r, s, t, w, y"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The older 20-letter alphabet"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In the 1980s eight more letters were added: **c, f, j, ñ, q, v, x, z**. They are used mostly for names (Carlos, Jollibee), borrowed words and science terms. Today the letters are named as in English, except **ng** and **ñ** (said *en-ye*, from Spanish)."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Old and new",
     "t": "Older teaching names the consonants with an \"a\" after them (ba, ka, da). You may still hear this. Modern Tagalog uses English letter names."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The five vowels",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog vowels are **short and pure**. English vowels often glide into a second sound (\"o\" becomes \"oh-oo\"). In Tagalog, say the vowel once and keep it steady."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Letter",
      "Sounds like",
      "Example word",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "a",
       "the a in father",
       "[[anák|noun]]  (a-NAK)",
       "child"
      ],
      [
       "e",
       "the e in bed",
       "[[kapé|noun]]  (ka-PE)",
       "coffee"
      ],
      [
       "i",
       "the ee in feet, but shorter",
       "[[iná|noun]]  (i-NA)",
       "mother"
      ],
      [
       "o",
       "the o in more, but pure",
       "[[otél|noun]]  (o-TEL)",
       "hotel"
      ],
      [
       "u",
       "the oo in food, but shorter",
       "[[úlo|noun]]  (U-lo)",
       "head"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Vowel sounds"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Accent marks",
     "t": "This course writes words with the same accent marks as the textbook (kapé, iná). The mark shows which syllable to stress. Everyday Tagalog is usually written without them. Lesson 2 explains all four marks."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "warn",
     "title": "Watch out",
     "t": "Do not shorten unstressed vowels into a vague \"uh\" the way English does. Each vowel keeps its own clear sound, even when it is not stressed."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Three special sounds",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "ng"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**ng** is one sound, like the end of the English word *sing*. The tricky part is that Tagalog also puts it at the **start** of words."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Push the back of your tongue up and back so it almost touches the roof of your mouth near the molars, make a nasal sound, then add the vowel that follows. Try it: say *sing*, hold the final \"ng\", then add a vowel straight after it: *ng-a*."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "ngayón",
       "en": "now, today",
       "parts": [
        [
         "ngayón",
         "now, today (nga-YON)",
         "other"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang mga batà",
       "en": "The children",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "batà",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Practise ng"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "ñ"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**ñ** sounds like **n + y** said together. Santo Niño sounds like *nin-yo*."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "r"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The Tagalog **r** is made by quickly tapping the tip of the tongue on the gum ridge behind the upper teeth, like a Spanish r but **not rolled or trilled**. It is the same flick as the \"tt\" in American *butter*. It is not the English r."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Practice words",
     "t": "[[Márso|noun]], [[Abríl|noun]] and [[restawrán|noun]] all contain the tapped r."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Diphthongs: two vowels, one beat",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A diphthong is a vowel glide that counts as **one syllable**. Tagalog has six: **ay, aw, ey, iw, oy, uy**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Glide",
      "Examples",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "ay",
       "[[áway|noun]]  (a-way), [[láway|noun]]  (la-way)",
       "fight, saliva"
      ],
      [
       "aw",
       "[[mabábaw|pred]]  (ma-ba-baw), [[áyaw|pred]]  (a-yaw)",
       "shallow, do not like"
      ],
      [
       "ey",
       "[[reyna|noun]]  (rey-na)",
       "queen"
      ],
      [
       "iw",
       "[[agiw|noun]]  (a-giw), [[bítiw|pred]]  (bi-tiw)",
       "cobweb, let go"
      ],
      [
       "oy",
       "[[káhoy|noun]]  (ka-hoy), [[amóy|noun]]  (a-moy)",
       "wood, scent"
      ],
      [
       "uy",
       "[[kasúy|noun]]  (ka-suy), [[badúy|pred]]  (ba-duy)",
       "cashew, dowdy"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The textbook's diphthong words"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "warn",
     "title": "The hard ones",
     "t": "English speakers often split **iw** and **uy** into two sounds, especially at the end of a word. Say them as one smooth glide: bítiw, not bi-ti-oo."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Every syllable counts",
     "t": "A Tagalog word is pronounced just as it is spelled, and each syllable is said separately and clearly."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Borrowed words in Tagalog spelling",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When a Spanish or English word enters Tagalog, it is respelled to match Tagalog sounds. If you learn the rules, you can decode a lot of vocabulary without a dictionary."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Spanish",
      "Becomes",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "hard c",
       "k",
       "calesa → [[kalésa|noun]] (rig)"
      ],
      [
       "soft c",
       "s",
       "circo → [[sírko|noun]] (circus)"
      ],
      [
       "ch",
       "ts",
       "lechon → [[litsón|noun]] (roast pig)"
      ],
      [
       "f",
       "p",
       "final → [[pinál|pred]] (final)"
      ],
      [
       "soft j",
       "h or s",
       "cajon → [[kahón|noun]] (box, drawer), jabon → [[sabón|noun]] (soap)"
      ],
      [
       "ll",
       "ly",
       "calle → [[kálye|noun]] (street)"
      ],
      [
       "q",
       "k",
       "maquina → [[mákina|noun]] (machine)"
      ],
      [
       "v",
       "b",
       "vapor → [[bapór|noun]] (ship)"
      ],
      [
       "z",
       "s",
       "lapiz → [[lápis|noun]] (pencil)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "From Spanish"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Becomes",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "soft c",
       "s",
       "cinema → [[sine|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "hard c",
       "k",
       "academic → [[akadémik|pred]]"
      ],
      [
       "ck",
       "k",
       "gimmick → [[gímik|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "ct",
       "k",
       "addict → [[ádik|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "qua or q",
       "kwa",
       "quality → [[kwáliti|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "long i",
       "ay",
       "driver → [[dráyber|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "f",
       "p",
       "traffic → [[trápik|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "v",
       "b",
       "believe → [[bilíb|pred]]"
      ],
      [
       "long o",
       "u",
       "approve → [[aprúb|pred]]"
      ],
      [
       "cle",
       "kel",
       "tricycle → [[tráysikel|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "tion",
       "syon",
       "institution → [[institusyón|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "x",
       "ks",
       "boxing → [[bóksing|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "j",
       "dy",
       "janitor → [[dyánitor|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "s at the beginning",
       "is",
       "sport → [[ísport|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "soft ch",
       "ts",
       "teacher → [[títser|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "hard ch",
       "k",
       "school → [[iskúl|noun]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "From English"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "More than one way",
     "t": "Because Tagalog borrowed from both Spanish and English, one English word can have two Tagalog forms. Quality can be **kwáliti** (from English) or **kalidád** (from Spanish). In everyday Tagalog, kalidád is the usual word; kwáliti is the book's example of how an English word gets respelled."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Names stay as they are",
     "t": "People, places and brand names usually keep their original spelling: Carlos, Jollibee, Leyte Gulf. Only ordinary words are respelled."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Everyday loanwords",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "These borrowed words are used constantly. Learn to say them as true Tagalog words, with every vowel clear."
    },
